<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Second Taifa of Mértola</h1><p class="st-meta">1144 CE – 1151 CE (approx.) · capital Mértola</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sufi-led rebel state in the Algarve whose leader Ibn Qasi rose against the Almoravids and opened the way for the Almohads.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-qasi">Ibn Qasi</a>, a former tax official turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> teacher, gathered disciples called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muridun">Muridun</a> in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a>. In 1144, as Almoravid rule weakened, his followers seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mertola">Mértola</a>, and he proclaimed himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> and mahdi.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The movement combined religious reform with rebellion against Almoravid taxes and jurists. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-qasi">Ibn Qasi</a> was allied for a time with Ibn al-Mundhir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silves">Silves</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidray-ibn-wazir">Sidray ibn Wazir</a> of Beja, but they soon turned against him. He was among the first to appeal to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a>, then sought support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso I of Portugal</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>His alliance with Portugal angered his own supporters, who killed him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silves">Silves</a> in 1151. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mertola">Mértola</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>, and his mystical writings were later studied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-arabi">Ibn Arabi</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1144 CE – 1151 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.47%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1144 CE – 1151 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mértola</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ibn Qasi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Sufi)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Portugal</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Muridun state of Ibn Qasi</dd></dl></aside></div>
